CHUNK({"chunk":"4d","module":"m4","topics":[{"id":"m4-am-intro","title":"Additive manufacturing basics","short":"Additive manufacturing","blurb":"Why additive manufacturing is used, the STL file, and the steps from CAD model to finished part.","source":"Lecture 14, pages 87–95","tabs":[{"title":"Why AM?","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>Additive manufacturing (AM)</b>, or 3D printing, builds a part layer by layer. It is the pinnacle in making <b>complex shapes</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Access to complex geometries</span><span class=\"chip\">Part consolidation</span><span class=\"chip\">Less scrap</span><span class=\"chip\">Light weighting</span></div>\n<p>It is used for the most intricate shapes and for <b>light weight, critical components</b>.</p>"},{"title":"The STL file","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>STL</b> stands for <b>STereoLithography</b> or <b>Standard Tessellation Language</b>.</p>\n<ul><li>STL uses <b>triangles</b> to describe the surfaces to be built.</li><li>Each triangle is described by <b>three points</b> and a <b>facet normal vector</b> pointing to the outward side.</li><li>A part can be built with different <b>layer thicknesses</b>, for example a teacup from its CAD model.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Process sequence","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Every AM part goes through three phases.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Digital phase</b> CAD model (digitising), conversion to STL, STL file manipulation, slicing, scanning strategy, build orientation.</li><li><b>Manufacturing phase</b> Transfer to the AM machine, machine setup, preparation of raw materials, selection of process parameters, the build.</li><li><b>Post processing phase</b> Removal from the machine, support removal, GD&amp;T analysis, testing and evaluation.</li></ol>\n<p>A <b>support structure</b> holds overhangs and sits between the part and the <b>build platform</b>; the part grows along the <b>build direction</b>.</p>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does AM stand for and what does it do?","back":"Additive manufacturing: builds parts layer by layer, ideal for complex, light weight shapes."},{"front":"What are the advantages of AM named in the course?","back":"Complex geometries, part consolidation, less scrap, light weighting."},{"front":"What does STL stand for?","back":"STereoLithography or Standard Tessellation Language."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-am-categories","title":"Seven categories of AM","short":"Seven AM categories","blurb":"The ASTM classification of AM processes: how each works, its strengths, materials and uses.","source":"Lecture 14, pages 96–105, 107","tabs":[{"title":"The seven","html":"<p class=\"lead\">The American Society for Testing and Materials (ASTM) classifies AM into <b>7 categories</b>.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Process</th><th>Material form</th><th>Energy source</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Material extrusion</td><td>Filament</td><td>Heat</td><td>FDM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vat photopolymerisation</td><td>Liquid resin</td><td>Light</td><td>SLA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Powder bed fusion</td><td>Powder</td><td>Laser or e-beam</td><td>SLS, SLM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Binder jetting</td><td>Powder and binder</td><td>Inkjet</td><td>Metal binder jetting</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Material jetting</td><td>Droplets</td><td>UV light</td><td>PolyJet</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Directed energy deposition</td><td>Wire or powder</td><td>Laser, e-beam or arc</td><td>LENS</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sheet lamination</td><td>Sheets</td><td>Adhesive or ultrasonic</td><td>LOM</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<div class=\"note exam\"><p>Choose the process by matching the product's needs: <b>material + accuracy + strength + volume + cost</b>.</p></div>"},{"title":"Vat, jetting, powder bed","html":"<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Vat photopolymerisation</h4><p>Liquid photopolymer resin cured by light (laser, projector or LCD): SLA, DLP, LCD printing. Strengths: high accuracy and complexity, smooth finish, large build areas. Uses: detailed prototypes, dental models, jewellery casting.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Material jetting</h4><p>Droplets deposited and cured layer by layer, like inkjet. Strengths: high accuracy, full colour, multiple materials in one part. Materials: photopolymers, polymers, waxes. Uses: high-resolution models, dental moulds.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Powder bed fusion</h4><p>A laser or electron beam fuses regions of a powder bed. Strengths: high complexity, powder supports the part, wide material range. Materials: nylon, TPU, Ti, Al, stainless steel, Inconel. Uses: aerospace, medical implants, lightweight automotive parts.</p></div></div>"},{"title":"Binder, DED, lamination","html":"<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Binder jetting</h4><p>Liquid binder joins powder particles. Needs sintering, infiltration or curing afterwards. Strengths: full colour, high productivity, wide materials (plastic, metal, ceramics, glass, sand).</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Directed energy deposition</h4><p>Powder or wire fed into a focused laser, e-beam or plasma arc and melted at the deposit point. Strengths: not limited by direction or axis, good for repair and adding features, highest single-point deposition rates. Uses: repairing turbine blades.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Sheet lamination</h4><p>Sheets bonded by adhesive, ultrasonic welding or heat, then cut to shape: LOM, UAM. Strengths: high build rates, low cost for non-metals, embedded components possible. Materials: paper, plastic sheets, metal foils.</p></div></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Who classifies AM into seven categories?","back":"ASTM (American Society for Testing and Materials)."},{"front":"Name the seven AM categories.","back":"Material extrusion, vat photopolymerisation, powder bed fusion, binder jetting, material jetting, directed energy deposition, sheet lamination."},{"front":"Vat photopolymerisation: material, energy, example?","back":"Liquid resin, light, SLA."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-fdm","title":"Fused deposition modelling (FDM)","short":"FDM","blurb":"How FDM prints, its three stages, and its limitations.","source":"Lecture 14, pages 106, 108–110","tabs":[{"title":"How FDM works","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Material extrusion, known as <b>fused deposition modelling (FDM)</b> or <b>fused filament fabrication (FFF)</b>, pushes material through a nozzle and deposits it layer by layer. It was developed by <b>Stratasys, USA</b>.</p>\n<ul><li>A <b>filament</b> of plastic is fed through a heated moving head, which melts and extrudes it.</li><li>It is deposited layer after layer in the desired shape, with support structures where needed.</li><li>The <b>platform lowers</b> after each layer.</li></ul>\n<p>Strengths: inexpensive, multiple colours, office-friendly, parts with good structural properties. Materials: thermoplastic filaments (<b>ABS, PLA</b>) and pellets. Uses: prototyping, tooling, low-cost functional parts.</p>"},{"title":"Three stages","html":"<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Design</b> CAD model, STL conversion, slicing to generate G-codes, choosing process parameters.</li><li><b>Printing</b> Heating the thermoplastic filament, extruding it, depositing layer by layer along with the support structure.</li><li><b>Post processing</b> Removing supports, then finishing.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Limitations","html":"<ul><li>Disadvantages lie in <b>build speed, accuracy and material density</b>.</li><li>A layer thickness of <b>0.078 mm</b> is only on the highest-cost machine, and it lengthens build time.</li><li>Sharp external corners are impossible: every corner or edge gets a <b>radius equal to the nozzle</b>.</li><li>The shape depends on the nozzle, acceleration and deceleration, and how the material behaves as it solidifies.</li><li>Speed depends on feed rate and plotting speed.</li><li>Properties are <b>anisotropic</b>: strength in the z-direction is measurably less than in the x-y plane.</li></ul>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are FDM and FFF?","back":"Fused deposition modelling / fused filament fabrication: heated filament is extruded layer by layer. Developed by Stratasys, USA."},{"front":"Typical FDM materials?","back":"Thermoplastic filaments such as ABS and PLA."},{"front":"Three FDM stages?","back":"Design (CAD, STL, slicing to G-code), printing, post processing."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[]});
